---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/base-layout.astro';
import PriceSheet from '../../components/price-sheet.astro';
import CommandPalette from '../../components/command-palette.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pricing in USD"
  description="Published US dollar prices for international clients: web, API, cloud, mobile and LLM security assessments with fixed turnaround and a free retest."
  path="/pricing/usd"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="page">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pricing · US dollars</p>
      <h1>International engagements</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        These are separate prices, not a currency conversion of the rupee sheet. The work has a
        different shape — different contract, different overlap hours, and a turnaround commitment
        that accounts for the gap between working days.
      </p>

      <PriceSheet currency="USD" />

      <div class="terms">
        <h2>What is different about working with us from abroad</h2>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Overlap hours.</strong> {site.overlapHours.usEastern} with US Eastern,{' '}
            {site.overlapHours.unitedKingdom} with the UK. Calls are booked in your timezone. There
            is a real person on the other end during those hours, not a ticket queue.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Contract and governing law.</strong> We will work under your master services
            agreement or ours. Governing law and dispute resolution are negotiable for non-Indian
            clients and we will not pretend otherwise to save a round of redlines.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Data handling.</strong> We act as a processor. Evidence is masked at capture and
            deleted {site.evidenceRetentionDays} days after release with written confirmation. Where
            the GDPR applies to you, a processing agreement with the standard clauses is available.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Credentials.</strong> Submitted through a one-time link into an encrypted vault.
            They never travel through email or chat, they expire, and they are destroyed on closure.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Payment.</strong> Fifty per cent on signature, fifty per cent before release.
            Bank transfer, and invoices carry whatever tax references your finance team needs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Independence.</strong> We do not build or operate the systems we test, and we do
            not take a stake in the companies we assess. This is what makes the report acceptable as
            third-party evidence.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="panel-sunk">
        <h2>On buying security work from India</h2>
        <p>
          You are going to ask, so here is the answer. The reason this costs less than a US or UK
          firm is the cost base, not the depth of the work. The methodology is the published one —
          OWASP WSTG, ASVS 5.0, MASVS, the LLM Top 10, NIST SP 800-115 — the tooling is the same
          tooling, and the report is written to be read by your buyer's security team.
        </p>
        <p>
          What you should check before hiring anyone, us included: read the sample report, ask who
          will actually do the work, ask what happens to your credentials and your evidence, and ask
          for the coverage matrix from a previous engagement with the client details removed. A firm
          that cannot show you those things is selling you a scanner subscription with a logo on it.
        </p>
        <p class="cta"><a class="button" href="/sample-report">Read the sample report</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CommandPalette />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h1 {
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  .terms {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-l);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .terms h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }

  .panel-sunk {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .panel-sunk h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
  }

  .cta {
    margin: var(--space-s) 0 0;
  }
</style>
